{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789380373,"nonce":"DpI1dQNO45VpQHbFKfdJd9/wbhw=","title":"Python: an exception raised inside an operator method does not leave the caller","message":"`Lowerer::dunder`, which every operator on a class instance goes\nthrough (`+`, `\u003c`, `len()`, truthiness, str), called the method and\nhanded the value back unchecked; only `method_on` (an explicit\n`obj.m()`) guarded the call. So a raise inside `__add__` set the\npending exception and returned null, and the caller kept going: a loop\napplying `acc + a` after `a` became None printed every remaining\niteration and raised at the end, where CPython leaves at the first.\n\n    for i in range(5):\n        print(\"iter\", i)\n        acc = acc + a\n        if i == 1:\n            a = None\n\nprinted iter 0..4 before the AttributeError.\n\n`dunder` now guards by the target's name like `method_on`, so a method\nthe non-raising analysis clears costs nothing and any other is checked.\nThe generated hooks (`guards` off) are unchanged; their callers check\nafter them. Conformance case `exceptions/raised_from_operator.py`.\n\nAlso fixed there: `arithmetic` boxed the right operand to Object before\n`dunder` unboxed it to the parameter's type, one box allocation and a\ntype check per `+` on a typed operand. `dunder` takes typed operands\nnow.","files":null}]}